.TH smi-create-db "1" "November 2026" "SMI Toolset" "SMI Base Command"
.SH NAME
smi-create-db \- Create an empty database, on a PostgreSQL server or as H2, Derby or SQLite files, ready for Liquibase and applications
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B smi-create-db
.B h2|derby|sqlite|postgres
.I NAME
.RI [ VARIABLE = value ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
.B smi-create-db
creates an empty database of the type given as the sub command, with the users and passwords it
needs, and prints, on standard output, the
.B SMI_DB_
variables to connect to it, as
.B export
lines for
.B eval
or a file to source. These are the variables
.BR smi-liquibase (1)
reads, so the database structure is applied next with no further setting.
.PP
.B postgres
creates the database on a server.
.BR h2 ,
.B derby
and
.B sqlite
create it as files, in the current directory or in
.BR SMI_DB_DIR .
The file databases are created by connecting to them once through
.BR smi-liquibase ,
with the driver of the
.I jdbc
directory of the SMI lib directory, so the database is made by the same driver that later uses
it.
.PP
A database that already exists is never touched: the command stops with an error before it
changes anything.
Everything but the variables goes to standard error.
.SH SUB COMMANDS
.TP
.B h2
Creates
.IB DIR / NAME .mv.db
with the user
.B SMI_DB_USER
and the password
.BR SMI_DB_PASSWORD ,
default
.B sa
and a new random password. H2 makes the first user of a new database its administrator.
Needs the
.B h2
package.
.TP
.B derby
Creates the embedded Derby database directory
.IB DIR / NAME .
Derby runs without authentication by default, so the user
.B app
is printed only to select the default schema
.BR APP ,
and the password is empty. Needs the
.B derby
package and Java 21 or newer.
.TP
.B sqlite
Creates
.IB DIR / NAME .db .
SQLite has no users, so user and password are empty. Needs the
.B sqlitejdbc
package.
.TP
.B postgres
Creates the database
.IB NAME _ ENVIRONMENT
with the application user
.IB ENVIRONMENT NAME
and the Liquibase user
.IB ENVIRONMENT NAME liquibase ,
by running the SQL of
.BR smi-postgresql-create-db-sql (1)
with
.BR psql (1)
as an administrator of the server, with the same privileges as described there.
It first checks that neither the database nor the users exist. Needs
.BR psql .
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I NAME
Lower case letters, digits and underscores, not starting with a digit.
.TP
.IR VARIABLE = value
Sets one of the variables below for this run, the same as setting it in the environment before
the command. Only the variables of the sub command are accepted, anything else is an error.
The value is taken as it is, nothing in it is run.
.SH VARIABLES
.SS h2, derby, sqlite
.TP
.B SMI_DB_DIR
Directory of the database files, created when missing, default the current directory.
The printed URL holds its absolute path, so the database is found from any directory.
.TP
.B SMI_DB_USER
.B h2
only: the user, default
.BR sa .
.TP
.B SMI_DB_PASSWORD
.B h2
only: the password, default a new one from
.BR smi-password-gen (1).
.SS postgres
.TP
.B SMI_DB_HOST
Server host, default
.BR localhost .
Set empty,
.BR SMI_DB_HOST= ,
to connect through the local Unix socket, for peer authentication as the
.B postgres
system user.
.TP
.B SMI_DB_PORT
Server port, default
.BR 5432 .
.TP
.B SMI_DB_ADMIN_USER
The administrator that creates the database and the users, default
.BR postgres .
.TP
.B SMI_DB_ADMIN_PASSWORD
Its password, passed to
.B psql
as
.BR PGPASSWORD ,
so it is not visible in the process list. Unset,
.B psql
uses
.IR ~/.pgpass ,
peer authentication, or asks.
.TP
.B SMI_DB_ENVIRONMENT
The SMI standard profile:
.BR local ,
.BR dev ,
.BR ci ,
.BR test ,
.B prelive
or
.BR live ,
default
.BR test .
See
.BR smi-postgresql-create-db-sql (1)
for their meaning.
.TP
.BR SMI_DB_APP_PASSWORD ", " SMI_DB_LIQUIBASE_PASSWORD
The passwords of the new users, default new random ones, see
.BR smi-postgresql-create-db-sql (1).
.SH OUTPUT
.PP
Shell
.B export
lines, single quoted, safe for
.B eval
whatever the password holds:
.TP
.B SMI_DB_TYPE
The sub command.
.TP
.B SMI_DB_NAME
The database name.
.TP
.B SMI_DB_URL
The JDBC URL.
.TP
.BR SMI_DB_USER ", " SMI_DB_PASSWORD
The user that changes the structure, the one
.B smi-liquibase
connects as: the Liquibase user for
.BR postgres ,
the only user otherwise.
.TP
.BR SMI_DB_APP_USER ", " SMI_DB_APP_PASSWORD
The user of the application: the application user for
.BR postgres ,
the same as
.B SMI_DB_USER
otherwise.
.TP
.BR SMI_DB_HOST ", " SMI_DB_PORT
.B postgres
only.
.PP
The output holds the passwords. Keep it in a file only the owner can read, or move the passwords
to where the application reads them, and remove it.
.SH DATABASE TOOLS AND WORKFLOW
.PP
The database tools of the toolset hand over to one another through the
.B SMI_DB_
variables, so each step needs no setting the previous step did not print.
.TP
.BR smi-download-package "(1), " smi-install-package (1)
Install the tools:
.B liquibase
for
.IR /opt/liquibase ,
and the JDBC driver of the database,
.BR pgjdbc ,
.BR h2 ,
.B derby
or
.BR sqlitejdbc ,
into the
.I jdbc
directory of the SMI lib directory.
.TP
.B smi-create-db
Creates the empty database and its users, and prints the variables.
.TP
.BR smi-postgresql-create-db-sql (1)
Prints the SQL that
.B smi-create-db postgres
runs, for a server where an administrator reviews or applies the SQL by hand instead.
.TP
.BR smi-password-gen (1)
Generates the passwords.
.TP
.BR smi-liquibase (1)
Applies, checks and reverts the structure and data changes, from XML, YAML, JSON or SQL
changelogs, as the user in
.BR SMI_DB_USER .
.TP
.BR smi-query-db (1)
Runs SQL, one statement, a script or interactively, as any user of the database, to check the
result of a migration, look at data or run reports.
.PP
The main flow, from nothing to a database an application can use:
.IP 1. 4
Install Liquibase and the driver, once per machine:
.RS
.nf
smi-download-package liquibase pgjdbc
smi-install-package liquibase pgjdbc
.fi
.RE
.IP 2. 4
Create the database and keep its variables:
.RS
.nf
umask 077
smi-create-db postgres shop SMI_DB_HOST=db.example.com > shop.env
.fi
.RE
.IP 3. 4
Apply the structure, from the directory of the changelogs:
.RS
.nf
\&. ./shop.env
smi-liquibase update
smi-liquibase status --verbose
smi-query-db postgres "SELECT tablename FROM pg_tables WHERE schemaname = 'public'"
.fi
.RE
.IP 4. 4
Give the application
.BR SMI_DB_URL ,
.B SMI_DB_APP_USER
and
.BR SMI_DB_APP_PASSWORD ,
for example as the Spring Boot
.BR SPRING_DATASOURCE_URL ,
.B SPRING_DATASOURCE_USERNAME
and
.BR SPRING_DATASOURCE_PASSWORD .
Later changes are new changelogs and another
.BR "smi-liquibase update" .
.PP
A file database follows the same flow, without a server, for development, tests or a local
application:
.RS
.nf
eval "$(smi-create-db sqlite shop SMI_DB_DIR=data)"
smi-liquibase update
smi-query-db sqlite "SELECT name FROM sqlite_master WHERE type = 'table'"
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The database was created and the variables printed.
.TP
.B 1
Wrong arguments, an unknown variable, an invalid name, a missing driver or
.BR psql ,
a database that already exists, or a failed creation; the error of the database is on standard
error.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
An H2 database in the current directory, with the structure applied right away:
.RS
.nf
eval "$(smi-create-db h2 trial)"
smi-liquibase update
.fi
.RE
.PP
A Derby database in another directory:
.RS
.nf
smi-create-db derby orders SMI_DB_DIR=/srv/data > orders.env
.fi
.RE
.PP
A live PostgreSQL database on the local server, as the
.B postgres
system user through the socket:
.RS
.nf
sudo -u postgres smi-create-db postgres shop SMI_DB_HOST= SMI_DB_ENVIRONMENT=live > shop_live.env
.fi
.RE
.PP
A remote server with the administrator password from the environment:
.RS
.nf
read -rs SMI_DB_ADMIN_PASSWORD; export SMI_DB_ADMIN_PASSWORD
smi-create-db postgres shop SMI_DB_HOST=db.example.com SMI_DB_ADMIN_USER=admin > shop_test.env
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I derby.log
Derby writes its log into the current directory, as it always does.
.TP
.IB NAME .trace.db
H2 writes it next to the database when it logs an error; the first one is the check of Liquibase
for its lock table in the still empty database, and is harmless.
.PP
Liquibase can not export the data of a Derby database with
.BR generate-changelog ;
the Derby procedure
.B SYSCS_UTIL.SYSCS_EXPORT_TABLE
writes a table as CSV, run through
.BR "smi-liquibase execute-sql" .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR psql (1),
.BR smi-download-package (1),
.BR smi-install-package (1),
.BR smi-liquibase (1),
.BR smi-neo4j-create-db (1),
.BR smi-password-gen (1),
.BR smi-postgresql-create-db-sql (1),
.BR smi-query-db (1)
